## Our Influences

GetToKnow.You did not begin with us. It builds on ideas that thoughtful people have been developing for generations about friendship, trust, curiosity, and human relationships.

One of the greatest pioneers was **Dale Carnegie**, whose classic book *How to Win Friends and Influence People*, first published in **1936**, introduced millions of people to the enduring value of showing genuine interest in others, listening well, and helping people feel valued.

Nearly ninety years later, those principles remain just as relevant.

We are not trying to reinvent them. Instead, we are exploring how they can be practised in a connected world—where conversations often begin online, cross cultures and languages, and are increasingly supported by artificial intelligence.

If Dale Carnegie helped a generation understand the timeless principles of building relationships, our hope is to help a new generation put those principles into practice using the tools and opportunities of the twenty-first century.

Our goal is simple: to build practical tools, communities, and learning experiences that help people turn timeless relationship principles into everyday habits. If our work encourages even a small number of people to become better listeners, more curious conversationalists, and better friends, then we will have achieved something worthwhile.

We gratefully acknowledge the pioneering work of Dale Carnegie, together with the many researchers, educators, and practitioners whose ideas continue to inspire us.

*GetToKnow.You is an independent initiative. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or associated with Dale Carnegie, the Dale Carnegie organization, or its training programmes.*
